\image(figures/johnson_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual negative-cycle relaxations continue decreasing on the detection pass.)
\alttext(Four consecutive target potentials are minus one, minus two, minus three and minus four; no heap search follows.)

The kernel now calculates in long double and checks produced arithmetic. It
rejects nonfinite intermediates, negative rounded reduced weights and restored
distances outside finite double range. It never clamps a negative reduced weight
to zero. Finite conversion may round a tiny result to zero. This is rounded
machine arithmetic, without an exact-real negative-cycle certificate or
tolerance model. Long double precision depends on the platform. A frozen
old-kernel comparison on this toolchain shows why the guards matter: double
saturation previously missed the extreme negative cycle with an isolated third
vertex, and finite path overflow could resemble an unreachable result.

> Measure preparation separately from repeated search

A directed path with every edge of weight −1 has no cycle. Ascending input order
propagates all potentials in one productive pass, followed by an unchanged pass.
Reverse order propagates only one more path edge per pass, taking n passes. The
graph and distances are identical. From source s, Dijkstra visits exactly n−1−s
edges, so all sources together inspect n(n−1)/2 edges. Returned finite entries
number n(n+1)/2, while matrix initialization always writes n² cells. The table
measures these operations rather than wall-clock time.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/johnson_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, asc, rev, search;
    std::print("| n | ascending BF checks | reversed BF checks | Dijkstra checks | matrix cells | restored " "cells |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n = 4; n <= 128; n *= 2) {
        const auto a =
            dm::algorithms::johnson_analyze(n,
                                            dm::algorithms::johnson_path_edges(
                                            n));
        const auto b =
            dm::algorithms::johnson_analyze(n,
                                            dm::algorithms::johnson_path_edges(
                                            n, true));
        if (a.result.distance != b.result.distance ||
            a.work.dijkstra_checks != n * (n - 1) / 2 ||
            a.work.potential_checks != 2 * (n - 1) ||
            b.work.potential_checks != n * (n - 1))
            throw std::runtime_error("work identities");
        const auto &w = a.work;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, w.potential_checks,
                   b.work.potential_checks, w.dijkstra_checks, w.matrix_cells,
                   w.restored_cells);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        asc.push_back(std::log2(w.potential_checks));
        rev.push_back(std::log2(b.work.potential_checks));
        search.push_back(std::log2(w.dijkstra_checks));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, asc, "BF asc", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, asc, "", {37, 99, 235},
                                                      4).line(x, rev, "BF rev",
                                                              {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, rev, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4).line(x, search, "search", {5, 150, 105}, 2).points(x, search, "", {5, 150, 105}, 4).title("Same path, different potential preparation work").x_label("log2(vertices)").y_label("log2(actual edge checks)").y_limits(2, 16).save_svg("build/johnson_work.svg");
}
```
