| n | ascending BF checks | reversed BF checks | Dijkstra checks | matrix cells | restored cells |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 4 | 6 | 12 | 6 | 16 | 10 |
| 8 | 14 | 56 | 28 | 64 | 36 |
| 16 | 30 | 240 | 120 | 256 | 136 |
| 32 | 62 | 992 | 496 | 1024 | 528 |
| 64 | 126 | 4032 | 2016 | 4096 | 2080 |
| 128 | 254 | 16256 | 8128 | 16384 | 8256 |
\image(figures/johnson_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Edge order changes potential work while repeated-search work stays fixed.)
\alttext(On base-two logarithmic axes, ascending-order potential work grows linearly in n; reversed preparation and all-source search counts grow quadratically.)

For n vertices and m directed edges, the potential phase costs O(nm). This lazy
binary heap can retain O(m+1) entries per source, including parallel-edge
alternatives; its logarithm is therefore log(m+2). A bound covering
empty/disconnected multigraphs and matrix storage is O(n²+nm+n(m+1)log(m+2)),
with O(n²+n+m) storage before tracing. For simple graphs, log(m+2) is
O(log(n+1)). The legacy shorthand O(nm+nm log n) omits the n² matrix term and
needs qualifications for parallel edges. Sparse input does not make this full
matrix output sparse.

> What the checks establish

Analyze returns owned output, potentials, reduced edges and work; trace
additionally owns the input graph and actual scalar operations. There are no
caller views or shared mutable state. Defaults cap complete n+m input records,
complete n² distance cells and online actual events at one million each. Zero
disables an optional ceiling. Complete validation/admission precedes matrix and
graph copies; each event is admitted before its operation. A failed call throws
without exposing a partial result. Untraced calls ignore the event limit, and
the legacy interface disables optional ceilings. Limits count records, excluding
vector capacity, byte counts, allocator overhead and peak RAM.

Seventeen native cases pass with sanitizers and the normal test runner. An
independent rational Floyd–Warshall and exact event/heap oracle checks 5,416
graphs, 423,252 actual operations, 2,427 global negative-cycle cases and 7,267
stale pops. Frozen comparisons preserve 194,749 matrix cells bitwise on 5,001
integer/dyadic inputs. In 1,000 decimal-weight acyclic controls, wider
arithmetic changes 5,491 cells by at most about 1.34e−15 on this toolchain;
bitwise preservation for arbitrary decimals is not claimed. The examples are
controlled graph problems, so no external dataset is needed. Together they show
how potentials enable repeated nonnegative searches, and why edge order, output
size and numerical domain remain separate concerns.

> References

Donald B. Johnson (1977), “Efficient algorithms for shortest paths in sparse
networks”, Journal of the ACM 24(1), 1–13,
[doi:10.1145/321992.321993|https://doi.org/10.1145/321992.321993]. Publication
metadata is verified; the publisher PDF was inaccessible in this run. The
telescoping and feasible-potential derivations can also be compared with Victor
Adamchik's
[CMU 15-451 lecture 13, pages 5–7|https://www.cs.cmu.edu/afs/cs/academic/class/15451-s14/www/LectureNotes/lecture13.pdf].
This implementation's lazy heap, explicit matrix term, arithmetic checks and
in-place pass accounting are specified above.
